WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.169
We have a ton of legislative news to dig into
today.

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.450
We'll be talking about the Equality Act.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.540
We will be talking about covid relief.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:15.049
But before talking about any of that massive
news late last night, the House of Representatives,

00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:19.980
thanks to Democrats mostly this was this was
Democrats who voted for this.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.699
Democrats in the House have passed the massive
voting rights and anti-corruption bill.

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:31.519
H.R. one Democratic Congressman Rokan to talk
to us about this when we interviewed him last

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:32.519
week.

00:00:32.519 --> 00:00:36.530
And this is not the first time that H.R. one
has passed in the House.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:41.520
It's the second time that it has passed, just
like the first time it passed.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.199
It's not clear how it passes the Senate and
we'll talk about that.

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:52.590
But this is an extensive bill, 800 pages,
all sorts of different things contained within

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.590
it.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:59.199
But the big items are a national system for
automatic voter registration.

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:02.410
This is something I've been talking about
for a decade.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:06.630
It's something that would dramatically increase
participation in elections.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.890
And it would ask right now you have to opt
in if you want to register to vote.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.000
The default is if you do nothing, you're not
registered.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.000
You have to opt in.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.619
The system proposed in H.R. one would create
an automatic voter registration system where

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:23.770
you have to opt out if you don't want to be
registered to vote.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:24.770
And why would you do that?

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:30.020
If you don't want to vote, you just wouldn't
vote and most people would just remain registered.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:36.250
The bill would require every state to put
online options for voter registration, correcting

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:41.680
your voter registration, canceling or designating
a party affiliation, things that in other

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.720
countries are just obvious that you have any
developed country should have these things.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:54.299
The bill would require at least 15 consecutive
days of early voting for federal elections,

00:01:54.299 --> 00:01:57.289
and early voting sites would have to be open
for at least 10 hours a day.

00:01:57.289 --> 00:02:02.009
This would short circuit and preempt many
of the state level voter suppression tactics

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:04.430
that Republicans have been using for a long
time.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:10.060
And the bill would also ban states from restricting
someone's ability to vote by mail.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.310
And it will also require states to prepay
postage on return envelopes for mail in voting

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.760
so it doesn't cost people money to vote by
mail.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:27.960
The bill would ban voter roll purges and also
would ban the use of non forward able mail

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.640
as a way to remove voters from rolls.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:38.130
Every one of these elements goes directly
at a technique used by the right to suppress

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.230
the vote.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:46.420
And very interestingly, H.R. one establishes
a public financing of campaigns with small

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:47.420
donations.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.450
The federal government would do a voluntary
six to one match for candidates for president

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:58.490
and Congress, which means every dollar a candidate
raises from small donors.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:03.370
The federal government would match six times
over, and the maximum small donation that

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.840
would be matched is capped at two hundred
dollars.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.950
It's not funded by taxpayer dollars.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.760
The money comes from a four point seventy
five percent fee on criminal and civil fines,

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:18.230
fees, penalties and settlements with banks
and corporations that do corporate malfeasance.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.980
Very interesting way to structure and finance
this.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:26.580
And it also contains and again, we can't possibly
cover every line, but there are transparency

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.940
requirements for political advertising.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:34.650
In H.R. one, it would establish nonpartisan
redistricting commissions to end partizan

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.370
gerrymandering.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.510
Massive issue.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.580
Again, we've been talking about it for a decade.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:49.210
It would require presidential and vice presidential
candidates to disclose 10 years of tax returns.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.710
And we know directly what that's related to.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:56.910
It would stop members of Congress from using
taxpayer money to settle sexual harassment

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.520
or discrimination cases and so many other
things.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:04.690
Now, beautiful bill has passed the House.

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.540
How does it pass the Senate?

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.300
And the answer is it doesn't unless it's modified
significantly.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.550
But maybe something can pass.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:17.200
The question is why Democrats control the
Senate.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.930
Democrats voted for this in the House.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.770
Why can't Democrats pass it?

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:25.640
The answer is there are just too many moderates
in the Democratic Party, in the Senate, and

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.210
the majority is too slim.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.990
In fact, it's the slimmest possible majority.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:34.760
The Senate is 50/50 Democrats, Republicans
because Democrats are in the White House.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:40.700
Kamala Harris becomes the tie breaking vote
in the Senate when there is a 50 50 tie.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.470
But this requires 60 votes to pass the Senate.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:51.770
So, in other words, I'm unsure that if it
only required 50, it would pass the Senate,

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.910
but they need 60 votes anyway.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:58.400
Now, one idea that Democrats are thinking
about is amending.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:06.130
The Senate filibuster rules so that any democracy
reform legislation like H.R. one could pass

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:10.080
with a simple majority instead of 60 votes
now.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.940
That's great if Joe Manchin goes for it.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.900
And that's very much unclear if Jeanne Shaheen
goes for it.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:21.200
I don't know if this is a bill that Jeanne
Shaheen is interested in passing.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.210
So right now, the situation anyway is you
need 60 votes.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:34.330
Now, this is an issue on which there is almost
no overlap between Democrats and Republicans.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:35.610
We talk on this program.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:41.330
You know, some shows love to take these black
white positions like there's no relevant difference

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.700
between Democrats and Republicans.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:48.290
And the truth is that on some issues, there's
no relevant difference between Democrats and

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:49.290
Republicans.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.460
And on other issues, they couldn't be more
different.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:57.070
Democrats, at least in the House, are passing
H.R. one, which is a massive expansion in

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:58.070
voting rights.

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:03.110
It would permanently cement many temporary
expansions on the basis of coronavirus.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.660
That's Democrats, at least in the House.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:11.230
On the other hand, as we talked about on I
think it was Monday, Republicans have more

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:20.520
than 250 bills in 43 different states to suppress
the vote to make it more difficult to vote.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:28.350
So these are diametrically opposed priorities
on these issues between Democrats and Republicans,

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:32.930
Democrats doing everything they can to increase
participation in elections, Republicans doing

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:34.790
everything they can to decrease participation.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:38.850
Now, you can take a cynical view and say,
well, both sides are doing what they think

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.860
is best for them, for them.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.990
Democrats believe more people voting is good
for Democrats.

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:47.080
Republicans believe fewer people voting is
good for Republicans.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.920
They are both probably correct.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:53.950
The difference is one of them is actually
in line with the principles and values that

00:06:53.950 --> 00:07:00.050
this country is based on, which is not disenfranchizing
people giving people a voice, not putting,

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.350
not obstructing the ability of people to participate
in the democratic process.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:10.710
It's hard to argue that the country is based
on the values that are exhibited by two hundred

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:14.310
and fifty Republican bills in 43 states to
suppress the vote.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:18.470
So, yeah, sure, you can take the view that
it's quite simple and it's Democrats want

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:19.470
more people to vote.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.470
So they when Republicans want fewer people
to vote, so they win.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:26.200
One of those perspectives happens to be in
line with the foundation of this country.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:33.090
Now, as a very practical matter, in the immediate,
Republicans have a particularly pressing reason

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:39.820
to oppose H.R. one, which is that they're
winning the House back in twenty twenty two

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:44.510
could depend on whether something like H.R.
one passes or doesn't pass.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.419
And we'll talk more about that in the future.

00:07:47.419 --> 00:07:48.740
That's H.R. one.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.409
Let's now talk about covid stimulus

